{
 "id": "2023-pollmanns-political",
 "name": "Political Graffiti and Neighborhood Ratings",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Study 1 of Pollmanns, C., & Asbrock, F. (2023). \"If graffiti changed anything, it would be illegal.\" The influence of political graffiti on the perception of neighborhoods and intergroup attitudes. Frontiers in Psychology, 14, 1098105. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1098105\n\nContent note: some photos show right-wing extremist graffiti (slogans and symbols). It is shown for research only and is not endorsed.\n\nWhat participants do: participants are randomly assigned to one of three picture sets. After a neutral practice picture, they see nine photos of residential streets in a fixed order, each for 10 seconds. Each street carries right-wing graffiti, left-wing graffiti or no graffiti, so every participant sees three of each, and each street × version is seen by a third of the sample. After each photo they rate the neighborhood next to a thumbnail: three social-cohesion items (1–7), cold–warm, orderly–disorderly and safe–unsafe (11-point) and the residents' political orientation (11-point manipulation check). They then give their age, gender, their own political orientation (1–7) and answer an accuracy item.\n\nOriginal finding (Study 1, N = 199, German Prolific sample): participants rated a neighborhood's social cohesion higher when its graffiti matched their own political orientation and lower when it was counter-attitudinal. Analysis per street: Helmert-coded version (neutral vs. political; left vs. right) × political orientation (z) regression; the authors combined the six items into one cohesion factor (EFA).\n\nStimuli: the authors' own photographs and graffiti edits from OSF (https://osf.io/2cmwr/), used with credit to the authors. This template is removed if the authors object.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) English wording instead of the German original; instructions are paraphrased. (2) The control and accuracy items are simplified to one accuracy question. (3) A content warning on the first page and a debrief at the end were added. (4) Only Study 1 is replicated (Study 2 scale development and Study 3 dose-response are not included).",
